\title{
Likelihood Ratio Test
%%  ~~function to do ... ~~
}
\description{
This function compares the fit of two nested models of trait evolution with a loglikelihood-ratio statistic.
%%  ~~ A concise (1-5 lines) description of what the function does. ~~
}
\usage{
LRT(model1, model2, echo = TRUE, plot=TRUE, ...)
}
%- maybe also 'usage' for other objects documented here.
\arguments{
  \item{model1}{
The most parameterized model. A fitted object from an mvMORPH model.
%%     ~~Describe \code{model1} here~~
}
  \item{model2}{
The second model under comparison (fitted object).
%%     ~~Describe \code{model2} here~~
}
  \item{echo}{
Whether to return the result or not.
%%     ~~Describe \code{echo} here~~
}

  \item{plot}{
Plot the distribution of the LRT statistic (under the null and alternative for simulation based approach with \code{mvgls} model fit.)
%%     ~~Describe \code{echo} here~~
}

\item{...}{
Options to be passed through (see details).
%%     ~~Describe \code{echo} here~~
}

}
\details{
The LRT function extracts the log-likelihood of two nested models to compute the loglikelihood-ratio statistic which is compared to a Chi-square distribution. Note that if the models are not nested or in high-dimensional settings (i.e., when the number of variables \emph{p} is larger than \emph{n}), the LRT can be used when bootstrapping or simulation-based distributions are used (e.g., Lewis et al. 2011).
This can be achieved using the \code{simulate} function (see examples below).

The various \emph{arguments} that can be passed through \bold{"..."}:

\bold{"nsim"} - The number of boostrap replicates used for estimating the null distribution.

\bold{"nbcores"} - The number of cores used to speed-up the computations (uses the 'parallel' and 'pbapply' packages).

\bold{"alternative"} - If \code{TRUE} the LR distribution is estimated assuming that \emph{model2} is the generative model. Default \code{FALSE}.

\bold{"parametric"} - If \code{TRUE} the null distribution is estimated using simulations. By default the distribution is estimated by bootstrapping (\code{parametric=FALSE}).

\bold{"REML"} - When \code{TRUE} the likelihood ratio (LR) and the null distribution are estimated using the restricted maximum likelihood (REML). When set to \code{FALSE}, these are estimated using the full likelihood with parameters estimated by REML. Using \code{REML=FALSE} allows models fitted with REML and with different fixed effects to be compared (see e.g., Verbyla 2019). Default \code{FALSE}.

%%  ~~ If necessary, more details than the description above ~~
}
\value{

\item{pval }{The p-value of the LRT test (comparison with Chi-square distribution).}
\item{ratio }{The LRT (Loglikelihood-ratio test) statistic.}
\item{ddf   }{The number of degrees of freedom between the two models.}
\item{model1   }{Name of the first model.}
\item{model2   }{Name of the second model.}
\item{dist    }{LR distribution (e.g., \emph{model1} as generative model). Only when simulations or bootstrap are used to generate the distribution.}
\item{dist_alt  }{LR distribution assuming \emph{model2} as the generative model. Only when the distribution is generated by simulations or bootstrap, and \code{alternative=TRUE}.}

